New:
```ts
import {answeringOrchestrator, delegatingTo, listedConversation, socketSandbox} from './wsTestSupport.js';
...
import type {SessionCursor, SessionListOptions, SessionStore} from '../types/sessionStore.js';
```

Wait — is `WebSocket` still used? Yes: `const clients: WebSocket[]`... no, that moves to the sandbox. `askAfterConnect` uses `new WebSocket(url, ...)` and `clients.push(client)` ✓ still used.

`AddressInfo` ✓ (startEngine), `Express` ✓, createServer ✓, HAL_WS_SUBPROTOCOL ✓, InMemorySessionStore ✓ (tests), setLogger ✓, SessionCursor ✓ (line 112), SessionListOptions ✓ (seen decl), SessionStore ✓.

The `Frame` interface keeps `sessions?: Array<{...}>` — unchanged.

Now the tricky part: `const {servers, clients, drain} = socketSandbox();` — and `logLines` decl. Then beforeEach/afterEach.

Then askAfterConnect restructure:
```ts
const askAfterConnect = async (url: string, outbound: unknown): Promise<{connected: Frame; frames: Frame[]}> => {
  const client = new WebSocket(url, [HAL_WS_SUBPROTOCOL, 't']);
  clients.push(client);
  const frames: Frame[] = [];
  let connected: Frame = {type: ''};
  await new Promise<void>(resolve => {
    client.on('message', raw => {
      const frame = JSON.parse(String(raw)) as Frame;
      if (frame.type === 'pong') {
        resolve();
        return;
      }
      if (frame.type !== 'connected') {
        frames.push(frame);
        return;
      }
      connected = frame;
      client.send(typeof outbound === 'string' ? outbound : JSON.stringify(outbound));
      client.send(JSON.stringify({type: 'ping', timestamp: 1}));
    });
  });
  return {connected, frames};
};
```

Hmm wait — `no-nested-if`: none. `prefer-early-return`: the arrow function passed to `client.on` now: two guard ifs each returning, then the flat happy path at the end — nothing dangles after the big if. Will prefer-early-return flag "a function whose body is one big if"? The body has multiple statements (frames decl etc.) — no. ✓

Behavior check vs original: original order — connected → assign+send+ping+return; pong → resolve+return; else push. New order — pong → resolve; not-connected → push; connected → assign+send+ping. Since a frame's type is exactly one value, the branches are disjoint; ordering is irrelevant. The `ping` is still sent after `outbound` on connect, so the pong barrier still guarantees ordering. ✓

One subtlety: in the ORIGINAL, `connected = frame` is assigned on EVERY 'connected' frame. Only one connected frame arrives per connection. ✓ same.

